From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 16:36:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ci: activate the build workflow

`ci/ci.yml` has been inert since it was added: GitHub only runs workflows
under `.github/workflows/`, so nothing compiled this package on a push and
publish.yml's own build and test were the only automated gate it had. A
broken commit was therefore caught at release time, which is the most
expensive moment to find one. Moved into place, exactly as ci/README.md
prescribed.

It builds and tests on every push and pull request: `npm ci`, `npm run
build`, `npm run test-cov`, with coverage uploaded as an lcov artifact and
deliberately not gated.

Verified before activating, with the same commands the job runs: `npm ci`
clean, `npm run build` clean, `npm run test-cov` exits 0 with 4/4 tests
passing and coverage.lcov produced. ci/README.md's "will it pass today"
answer still holds.

Seven places asserted the workflow was dormant, and all of them are now
wrong, so none of them stay:

- the file's own header, which said DORMANT
- publish.yml, which called itself the only automated build and test, and
  named the old ci/ci.yml path
- ci/README.md, framed entirely around activating it
- AGENTS.md, "`ci/` holds the dormant build workflow"
- STYLE-GUIDE.md, twice: the ci/COVERAGE.md row, and the claim that
  ci/README.md "describes a workflow that does not run"
- tools/check_prose.py, where the comment explaining why EXTRA_PAGES is
  empty rested on ci/README.md documenting "a dormant workflow"

The last one is why the sweep was repeated without a file-type filter: the
first pass looked only at .md, .yml and .json, and a stale claim in a .py
comment survived it.

publish.yml keeps its own build and test, and the header now says why that
is not redundant: nothing there reads ci.yml's result, so a dispatch
releases the ref it is given whether that commit's CI is green, red or
still queued.

ci/README.md keeps the two things worth outliving the move -- why a
coverage threshold set from the headline figure would lock in an illusion,
and that no job yet checks a generated scaffold actually installs -- and
records one thing found while activating: ci.yml pins no action, using
floating `@v4` tags where docs.yml and publish.yml pin full-length SHAs at
v7. Left as it was authored rather than changed under an activation commit.

